      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability of two independent events occurring together: rolling a number less than three on the first die and rolling a number greater than three on the second die.\n\nFirst, let's identify the possible outcomes for each die:\n- The first die is a six-sided die, so it can show any number from 1 to 6.\n- The second die is also a six-sided die, so it can show any number from 1 to 6.\n\nNow, let's determine the probability of each event:\n1. The probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:\n   - The numbers less than three are 1 and 2.\n   - There are 2 favorable outcomes (1 and 2) out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   - Therefore, the probability is \\(\\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\\).\n\n2. The probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:\n   - The numbers greater than three are 4, 5, and 6.\n   - There are 3 favorable outcomes (4, 5, and 6) out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   - Therefore, the probability is \\(\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nSince these two events are independent, the probability of both events happening together is the product of their individual probabilities:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{6}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
