      "text": "To find the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die.\n2. Determine the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die.\n3. Calculate the combined probability of both events happening independently.\n\n### Step 1: Probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die\nA six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. So, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n\nThe probability \\( P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\) is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die\nThe numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6. So, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n\nThe probability \\( P(\\text{second die} > 3) \\) is:\n\\[\nP(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Combined probability\nSince the dice rolls are independent events, the combined probability is the product of the individual probabilities:\n\\[\nP(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and } \\text{second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]",
